text
@Here is some C code that is supposed to translate a kMoriaSaveDB.pdb
file to/from the Umoria 5.5.2 save file format.

This program is released under the GPL (see
http://www.gnu.org/copyleft/gpl.html) because I used some source code
from another GPL program which I did not write.  (I did not really use
source code from Moria (which has a different License that is probably
incompatible with GPL.. you know, one of them is a morning person, the
other isn't, squeezes the toothpaste in the middle of the tube..
*both* of them leave hair in the sink..); just knowledge of how the
save files are laid out.)

I have tried it out, only on Linux, and only on one save file.  This
single data point works.  So, I am including this code in the source
distribution as a rudimentary example for interested people to
improve.  This code is "unsupported" - I will not act on bug reports
unless they are accompanied by a patch.  (If it does not work for your
platform, fix it and send me a patch.  If you do not know C, learn C
first, then fix it, then send me the patch; or talk another kMoria
user into fixing it.)

Use:

When converting FROM pdb file TO umoria save file, it needs an
existing pdb file, and it will create the umoria save file from
scratch. [1]

When converting TO pdb file FROM umoria save file, it needs an
existing umoria save file, AND, it also needs an existing pdb file
which will be MODIFIED. [2]  (Since there is no guarantee that this
program will work, better make a copy of this sacrificial pdb file in
case something goes wrong.)  Any old version of the save file will do,
as long as it is the same character. [3]

If you convert a pdb file to a umoria save file, and then convert it
right back to a pdb file, the two pdb files (old and new) should not
differ at all.  If they do differ, probably something is wrong.


[1] Well, except for the character background string ("You are the
first child of a Guildsman.  You are a well liked child." etc), which
I skipped (and replaced with some gibberish) and didn't get around to
not skipping.  If you want to tinker, feel free to make this work.

[2] Obviously this is not the "right" way for a conversion program to
act, but for a rough start it was certainly easier to do it this way.
PalmOS database files have a header, then some information about where
each record *is* and what the unique ids are, then all the records.
The records in the save database are all basically of constant length
(except for that character background string, which will be different
for different characters), so we can just reach into that file and
write new stuff into the records without really having to think about
the header.  If you want to do some advanced tinkering, you can revise
the program so that it just writes out a new database file from
scratch (just make sure to get the unique ids right; kMoria uses them,
not the record index number, to look up records).

[3] And if it is not the same character, the new character will be
stuck with the same character background string (and, at the moment,
the same top-scores list) as the old character.  That string is not
touched, because it is variable length and would require cleverness.

@
